From: dsanke <dsanke1997@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: fix bridge-fallback guard killing NPU RX binds

The host-destined exclusion added for the PPE bridge fallback checked
skb->pkt_type == PACKET_HOST.  On the NPU/WED and wired DMA RX paths the
skb is built by napi_build_skb() and pkt_type keeps its PACKET_HOST
default until eth_type_trans() classifies the frame later inside
__netif_receive_skb_core(), which happens after the driver already
invoked airoha_ppe_check_skb().  As a result every WLAN uplink
(STA->AP) frame looked host-destined, airoha_ppe_foe_prepare_bridge_subflow()
always returned false and the bridge-FDB fallback never ran.  Once the
flowtable entries for a STA are torn down on disconnect (FDB roaming
invalidation), a reconnecting STA's uplink flows could never be re-bound:
the flowtable/l2_flows path was gone and the FDB fallback was dead,
leaving uplink FOE entries UNB.  Downlink kept working because it binds
through the flowtable WDMA path.

Drop the pkt_type test and keep the real host-destined exclusion
(destination MAC equal to the bridge master's own MAC; the bridge FDB
also refuses to resolve local entries, so this is belt-and-suspenders).

Also skip the fallback when the FDB resolves the destination to the same
ingress interface (intra-BSS on an AP, or a port self-loop on a GDM
netdev): such traffic stays in the software/WLAN datapath and must not
be offloaded to a WDMA/GDM egress.

Signed-off-by: dsanke <dsanke1997@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c | 18 +++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
@@ -1016,9 +1016,15 @@ airoha_ppe_foe_prepare_bridge_subflow(struct airoha_ppe *ppe,
 	 * path for the bridge's own MAC, but make the exclusion explicit so
 	 * a stray FDB/PPE classification can never offload host-bound flows
 	 * into hardware and black-hole them.
+	 *
+	 * Note: skb->pkt_type cannot be used here.  On the NPU/WED and wired
+	 * DMA RX paths the skb is built by napi_build_skb() and pkt_type
+	 * keeps its PACKET_HOST default until eth_type_trans() classifies
+	 * the frame later in __netif_receive_skb_core(), i.e. after the PPE
+	 * check already ran.  Every uplink frame would therefore look
+	 * host-destined and the bridge fallback would never fire.
 	 */
-	if (skb->pkt_type == PACKET_HOST ||
-	    ether_addr_equal(eh->h_dest, master->dev_addr)) {
+	if (ether_addr_equal(eh->h_dest, master->dev_addr)) {
 		dev_put(master);
 		return false;
 	}
@@ -1078,6 +1084,15 @@ airoha_ppe_foe_prepare_bridge_subflow(struct airoha_ppe *ppe,
 		dev_put(master);
 		return false;
 	}
+	/* Same-interface forwarding (intra-BSS on an AP, or a port self-loop
+	 * on a GDM netdev) stays in the software/WLAN datapath: binding it to
+	 * a WDMA/GDM egress would fight the 802.11/bridge driver path.
+	 */
+	if (egress == skb->dev) {
+		rcu_read_unlock();
+		dev_put(master);
+		return false;
+	}
 	dev_hold(egress);
 	rcu_read_unlock();
 
